  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The NNDP, formed at Lagos in 1923 by Herbert Macaulay. The NCNC of 1944 was the first national party, not the first party.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Herbert Macaulay, often called the father of Nigerian nationalism. The other three led the NCNC, the AG and the NPC.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe. The party began as the National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons and later became the National Council of Nigerian Citizens.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The AG was the western party, led by Chief Obafemi Awolowo. Its base was the Yoruba-speaking west, and it became the opposition after 1959.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sir Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto, led the NPC. Tafawa Balewa was the party's deputy and became Prime Minister.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Malam Aminu Kano founded the NEPU as a radical northern party opposed to the emirs and the NPC establishment.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    NEPU opposed the NPC and the emirs from the left, which is why it allied with the NCNC in 1959. It was a party, not a pressure group.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1934, as a pressure group of the educated elite rather than a party. 1923 is the NNDP, 1944 the NCNC and 1951 the AG and NPC.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The nationalists used the press, petitions, strikes such as the general strike of 1945, and delegations. Armed rebellion was not the strategy of the party leaders.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Azikiwe founded the Pilot in 1937; it became the most effective nationalist newspaper in the country.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The strike lasted over a month and demonstrated organised mass action. The NCNC had been formed a year earlier, and independence came fifteen years later.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chief Anthony Enahoro moved the motion in the House of Representatives. It was carried in the south and rejected in the north, which deepened the crisis of 1953.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Kano riots followed the tour of northern leaders by southern politicians, and the London conference of 1953 produced the Lyttleton Constitution of 1954.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It created an elective seat for Lagos and Calabar, the first elections in Nigeria. The regions came in 1946 and the federal structure in 1954.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Richards created the North, West and East regions, with the Cameroons attached to the East. It did not introduce federalism in the full sense.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Macpherson created a Council of Ministers with Nigerian members holding portfolios, the first real share of executive power.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lyttleton divided power between the federal government and the regions, which is why 1954 is the date the paper attaches to Nigerian federalism.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Clifford, 1922. The Governor-General replaced the Governor as the head of the colonial administration.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1 October 1960. 1963 is the republic, 1959 the federal election and 1966 the first coup.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, the NPC deputy leader, led the coalition government from 1957 and became Prime Minister in 1960.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The NPC won the largest bloc but not a majority, so it combined with the NCNC. The coalition was an arithmetical necessity, not a constitutional rule.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The NPC, with 134 of the 312 seats. The NCNC took 89 and the AG 73, which is why the NPC had to look for a partner.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    NPC 134 plus NCNC 89 gave a comfortable majority. The AG went into opposition, which is why Awolowo became the Leader of the Opposition.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Awolowo, whose AG had lost the coalition arithmetic. The office is a mark of a parliamentary system, and the paper sometimes asks who held it.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1963, when the Republican Constitution replaced the Queen with a Nigerian President as head of state. 1960 is independence.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The President was ceremonial, and the Prime Minister held executive power. The executive presidency came with the 1979 Constitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1963, out of the Western Region, following minority demands. The twelve states replaced the regions in 1967.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The parties were regional, the coalition was fragile, and the census and election disputes fed the crisis of 1962–65. The First Republic had a constitution and a lively press.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Population determined seats and revenue, so the count was a struggle for power between the regions. The census was cancelled and repeated.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The disputed 1965 election in the west was followed by arson and killings known as “wetie”, and the disorder was one of the justifications the coup plotters used in January 1966.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    India and Ghana showed that independence was attainable, and Ghana's 1957 independence under Nkrumah was a direct inspiration to the Nigerian parties.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Charter's statement of self-determination was quoted back at Britain by nationalists, who asked why it applied in Europe and not in Africa.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti led the Abeokuta Women's Union and campaigned against taxation and the Alake. The other three are male nationalists.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons, because the Cameroons were administered with Nigeria until the 1961 plebiscite.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The regions gained self-government in residual matters, while defence, foreign affairs and currency stayed federal. Independence came in 1960.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They settled the composition of the federal parliament, the position of the regions and the date of independence, producing the 1960 Constitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The NPC, led by Ahmadu Bello, dominated the Northern Region. The AG was the west, the NCNC the east and the NNDP a Lagos party.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1923, for the three Lagos seats and one Calabar seat created by the Clifford Constitution. 1959 is the last election before independence.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Self-government and control of resources were the core demands. The nationalists did not seek a change of colonial master or an end to all taxation.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The 1960 Constitution was parliamentary: a Prime Minister responsible to parliament and the Queen represented by a Governor-General. The executive presidency came in 1979.
  ]

]

